---
import Learn from '../../layouts/Learn.astro';
import MemoryFlow from '../../components/learn/MemoryFlow.astro';
import BarChart from '../../components/learn/BarChart.astro';

// Learn article 2 (s91). Every statement about Claude Code is from Anthropic's
// "How Claude remembers your project" (code.claude.com/docs/en/memory), read on
// 2026-10-06: CLAUDE.md (you write) and auto memory (Claude writes), both loaded
// every session; auto memory in ~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory/, one per git
// repository, shared by its worktrees; the first 200 lines or 25KB of MEMORY.md
// load at session start, topic files are read during the session; machine-local,
// not shared across machines or cloud environments; /memory toggles it; CLAUDE.md
// target under 200 lines.
// The chart is measured on our own project on 2026-10-06 (sizes only): MEMORY.md
// 155 lines, 19,789 bytes; 182 memory files, 506,118 bytes in all, kept since
// 2026-04-28. Zaaheen's steps are the connect guide's (/docs/connect-claude-code/).
const OUTLINE = [
  { id: 'what', title: 'What Claude Code remembers' },
  { id: 'loads', title: 'What loads each session' },
  { id: 'limits', title: "What it doesn't carry" },
  { id: 'across', title: 'Across projects and apps' },
];
const DOCS = 'https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory';
---
<Learn
  path="/learn/does-claude-code-remember-between-sessions/"
  lede="Yes, within one project on one computer. Claude Code starts every session with a fresh context, then loads two kinds of notes: the CLAUDE.md files you write and the auto memory it writes itself. Neither follows you to another project's memory folder, another computer or another AI app."
  outline={OUTLINE}
>
  <section id="what">
    <h2>What Claude Code remembers</h2>
    <p>Anthropic's documentation (<a href={DOCS}>How Claude remembers your project</a>) describes
       two ways knowledge carries from one session to the next:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>CLAUDE.md files</strong> are instructions you write: how to build the project,
          rules to follow, things to avoid. They can sit in the project, in your user folder or
          be set by your organisation, and every one that applies is loaded at the start of each
          session.</li>
      <li><strong>Auto memory</strong> is notes Claude Code writes for itself as you work: your
          corrections, preferences and what it has learned about the project. When you say
          "remember that the tests need a local database", this is where it goes.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Both are plain text files you can open and edit. In a session, <code>/memory</code> lists
       them, opens them, and turns auto memory on or off.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="loads">
    <h2>What loads at the start of each session</h2>
    <p>Auto memory lives in a folder for each project,
       <code>~/.claude/projects/&lt;project&gt;/memory/</code>, with a file called MEMORY.md as its
       index. Only the first 200 lines or 25KB of that index, whichever comes first, are loaded when
       a session starts. The detailed notes sit in separate topic files that Claude Code opens when
       it needs them.</p>
    <p>We build Zaaheen with Claude Code every day, so we measured our own project's memory after
       five months of sessions:</p>

    <BarChart
      id="chart-claude-code-memory"
      title="One project's Claude Code memory after five months: kept vs loaded at the start"
      measure="Kilobytes"
      max={500}
      bars={[
        { label: 'All memory files', value: 494, note: 'KB' },
        { label: 'Loaded at start', value: 19, note: 'KB' },
        { label: 'Start-up limit', value: 25, note: 'KB' },
      ]}
      source={{ text: "Zaaheen's own project, measured on 6 October 2026: 182 memory files kept since 28 April 2026; a MEMORY.md index of 155 lines. The limit is from Anthropic's Claude Code documentation.", href: DOCS }}
    />

    <p>So Claude Code does remember, but most of what it knows is looked up on demand rather than
       read every time. Keeping the index short, with one line per memory, is what keeps the
       important things in view. Anthropic suggests keeping each CLAUDE.md under 200 lines for the
       same reason.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <h2>What it doesn't carry</h2>
    <p>The documentation is clear about the edges:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>One memory folder per project.</strong> All the worktrees of one repository share
          it, but a different repository has its own, so what Claude Code learned about you in one
          project isn't in the next.</li>
      <li><strong>One computer.</strong> Auto memory is machine-local and isn't shared across
          machines or cloud environments.</li>
      <li><strong>One app.</strong> Cursor, Codex, ChatGPT and Claude Desktop don't read Claude
          Code's memory folder.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For project rules, that is exactly right: they belong with the project. For facts about
       you, such as how you like answers, the tools you prefer or what you're working on this
       month, it means telling each project and each app again.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="across">
    <h2>Remembering you across projects and apps</h2>
    <p>A memory that lives outside Claude Code fills that gap. Claude Code can connect to tools
       through MCP, the standard way AI apps plug into tools and data, so it can save facts about
       you to a memory that every project, and your other AI apps, can read.</p>

    <MemoryFlow
      id="flow-claude-code-cursor"
      from="Claude Code"
      to="Cursor"
      fact="Prefers TypeScript strict"
      others={['Codex', 'ChatGPT']}
      caption="A preference Claude Code learns in one project is saved once, on your computer. Cursor, Codex and ChatGPT can recall it in any project."
    />

    <p>Zaaheen is one such memory, for Windows and Mac. You connect Claude Code once, with one
       command from Zaaheen's Agents tab, and it is then available in every Claude Code project
       (<a href="/docs/connect-claude-code/">step by step</a>). Your memories stay encrypted on
       your own computer.</p>
    <p>A good split: keep project rules in CLAUDE.md, let auto memory hold what Claude Code learns
       about the project, and keep what's true about you in one shared memory that every app
       reads.</p>
  </section>
</Learn>
